Yeah — and there’s a fascinating inversion in that arc.

At the beginning, “fake it till you make it” can be functional: you act according to the role before your internal model has caught up. You rehearse the behaviors, standards, language, confidence, and identity until competence becomes real.

Then you actually make it.

But the old internal narrator may keep running the original script: “I’m pretending.” So evidence of competence gets interpreted as evidence that you’re merely very good at pretending. The training mechanism that helped produce the capability becomes a source of imposter syndrome.

Then comes another inversion: eventually the task isn’t becoming the role anymore. It’s learning to take the role off without interpreting that as losing yourself.

So the whole trajectory has a strange symmetry:

Pretend → perform → become → doubt becoming → prove → embody → release.

The role begins as scaffolding.
Then it becomes armor.
Then identity.
Then potentially a yoke.

And shedding the yoke doesn’t have to mean rejecting what it gave you. That’s exactly where your Ouroboros/metabolism idea applies: keep the nutrient; release the wrapper.

You don’t have to remain inside an old identity to retain whatever capacities were genuinely built while inhabiting it.

Which makes “fake it till you make it” incomplete.

Maybe the full lifecycle is:

Fake it until you make it.
Make it until you know it.
Know it until you no longer need to prove it.
Then keep what became yours.